Classic pitfall: I escaped the single quotes inside the probe string as `\'` inside a JS single-quoted string literal within my run_code source, but... no wait, that's not it. The issue is this: inside my run_code TypeScript program I wrote the probe as a JS single-quoted string containing `\'canvas\'` — the escape `\'` inside a single-quoted JS string is valid and produces `'`. But looking at the generated /tmp/mkprobe.js output: the line contains `document.querySelector('canvas')` — the backslash has disappeared.

Ah, I get it — inside my run_code `code` I wrote the probe as a JS single-quoted string containing `\'`... the run_code program itself is a TS function body, and I wrote the string `const probe = '...\'canvas\'...'` inside it. That should be fine inside the run_code program — the TS string gets passed to tools.write as JSON, so the actual string content is `const probe = '<script>...querySelector(' + ...` no wait, that's not right either.

Let me think again. Inside my run_code program source:
```
const probe = '<script>(function(){...document.querySelector(\'canvas\')...';
```
The TS source of my run_code program contains `\'canvas\'` inside a single-quoted string → when the TS program is evaluated, the JS string contains `'canvas'` (with actual single quotes). Then tools.write writes that into /tmp/mkprobe.js. So /tmp/mkprobe.js contains the following:
```
const probe = '<script>...document.querySelector('canvas')...';
```
This is broken inside mkprobe.js: the single-quoted string terminates at the first `'` of `'canvas'`. That's exactly the error: `Unexpected identifier 'canvas'` at that position.

In other words, when writing a file that itself contains JS string literals with single quotes, I need to double-escape: in the run_code source, to produce `\'` in the file, I have to write `\\'`.

Simplest fix: rewrite mkprobe so the probe HTML is built with a template literal (backticks), and the probe itself uses single quotes — inside the template literal, single quotes don't need escaping. And the `</script>` inside a template literal is fine (it's a JS file, not HTML). Also, the `\\n` for the renderer line replacement: I wrote `\\n` in the run_code source inside a normal single-quoted string → it produces a literal `\n` (backslash + n) in the file, which inside mkprobe.js's single-quoted string gets interpreted as a newline. That part was correct.

Also, my cdpClient and pngStat files passed node --check, and mkprobe's file was written — but mkprobe failed at runtime. All three files exist; the probe content in mkprobe.js is broken. Let me rewrite mkprobe.js using a template literal for the probe string.

Another consideration: the probe's `</script>` sequence — inside the mkprobe.js file it's just a string, no problem. But in the final HTML, `</script>` inside a <script> block is what terminates the script — that's exactly the intent (closing tag for the probe's script). Good.

Rewrite mkprobe.js:
```js
const fs = require('fs');
let s = fs.readFileSync('/Users/alvin/claude-cowork/hangar20-dsh-ptc/hangar20.html','utf8');
const rline = 'const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true, powerPreference: "high-performance" });';
if (!s.includes(rline)) throw new Error('renderer line not found');
s = s.replace(rline, rline + '\nwindow.__renderer = renderer;');
const probe = `<script>
(function(){
  function grab(id){var e=document.getElementById(id);return e?e.innerText:null;}
  setTimeout(function(){
    try{
      var info={ok:true};
      var cv=document.querySelector('canvas');
      info.canvas=cv?{w:cv.width,h:cv.height}:null;
      var r=window.__renderer;
      if(r){
        var gl=r.getContext();
        var dbg=gl.getExtension('WEBGL_debug_renderer_info');
        info.gl={
          version:gl.getParameter(gl.VERSION),
          renderer:dbg?gl.getParameter(dbg.UNMASKED_RENDERER_WEBGL):null,
          isLost:gl.isContextLost(),
          calls:r.info.render.calls,
          points:r.info.render.points,
          tris:r.info.render.triangles,
          frame:r.info.render.frame
        };
      }
      info.hud={title:grab('title'),status:grab('status'),vForm:grab('vForm'),vCine:grab('vCine'),vFog:grab('vFog')};
      window.__probe=info;
    }catch(e){
      window.__probe={ok:false,err:String(e&&e.stack||e)};
    }
  },10000);
})();
<\/script></body>`;
```
Wait — inside a template literal, `<\/script>` — `\/` is not a valid escape inside a template literal! In template literals, only `$`, backtick, and backslash escapes are recognized, and `\/` becomes... actually, in template literals an unrecognized escape `\/` is... hmm, in untagged template literals invalid escapes like `\4` are an error, but `\/` is an "identity escape" — valid in both string literals and template literals (IdentityEscape is permitted). `\/` is valid (a non-escape character escaped by a backslash is allowed in template literals — yes, IdentityEscape :: any SourceCharacter but EscapeCharacter; `/` is not an escape character, so `\/` → `/`). So `<\/script>` inside a template literal works and produces `</script>`.

But wait — why did I write `<\/script>` in the first place? In an HTML file, a literal `</script>` inside a script element's content terminates the script element — but here the probe's script is the final script before `</body>`, and its content ends with `</script></body>` — no wait. The HTML structure is: `<script> ...probe code... </script></body>`. The probe code itself contains the string `'<\/script></body>'` — no wait, that's wrong. The probe code is the actual script content, and after it comes the real closing tag `</script>`. The HTML parser's raw text: script content runs until the first `</script>` sequence. My probe's JS code contains `window.__probe = ...` — it doesn't contain a literal `</script>` anywhere inside the code. The closing `</script>` after `})();` is the real terminator. So I don't need `\/` at all — I just write:

```
s = s.replace('</body>', '<script>' + probeJs + '</script></body>');
```

Here probeJs is the plain JS code with no `</script>` inside it.

So inside the run_code program:
```ts
const probeJs = `(function(){ ...code containing 'canvas' etc... })();`;
const probeBlock = '<script>' + probeJs + '<' + '/script></body>';
```
Note: inside a template literal, `'</script>'` — a single-quoted string inside the template literal is fine; the sequence `</script>` in the JS file is fine (it's a JS file, and the browser is not parsing this JS as HTML). And when written to HTML, the parser sees `<script>` + code + `</script>` — the code must not contain `</script>` — and it doesn't.

Also, inside the template literal I must avoid `${` sequences — my probe code doesn't have any.

One more check: the probe's `setTimeout(..., 10000)` — under CDP without virtual time, real 10 seconds. My client waits 10.5 seconds after load. Good.

And the client: after load, waits 10.5s → probes → 2 shots → key c → 3.5s → shot → key f → 4.5s → shot → key g → 2s → shot. Total ≈ 10.5+2 (shot overhead) +3+3.5+4.5+2 ≈ 26 seconds + load time (CDN fetch under real network ~1-3s, no virtual time). Well within the 60-second timeout. Run it in the foreground with timeoutMs 55000.

Also, I need to launch Chrome first as a background job. Let me reorganize:

Program 1 (this one): rewrite mkprobe.js (with a clean probe), run node /tmp/mkprobe.js, syntax-check everything again.
Program 2: launch Chrome in the background with remote debugging; then run the client in the foreground with timeout 55s; then run pngstat on the shots; print the results.

Actually, I can do it all in one program: bash background to launch chrome (run_in_background:true returns a jobId), then in the same program run the client via bash (foreground, timeout 55000), then the pngstat bash, and finally job_kill the chrome job. Sequence it properly: the client's first fetch might fail if Chrome isn't up yet — the client will fail on the /json/list fetch if the port isn't up. Add a retry loop to the client: try /json/list up to 15 times at 1-second intervals. I'll patch the client to add a small retry. Actually, more simply: in the program, after launching the background chrome, sleep 3 seconds, then run the client.

Now let me write it. Also, kill any stale Chrome from the previous run (pkill -f "Google Chrome" might kill the user's real Chrome! Careful — use lsof on port 9222 only, and also kill by the exact headless profile marker: pkill -f "Chrome-headless" — hmm, that could match the user's Chrome if it were using a "Chrome-headless" profile directory name... The default profile directory for --headless=new is "Chrome" not "Chrome-headless"... in fact, in the earlier logs I saw the "Chrome-headless" directory (Chrome-headless/scoped_dir...) — that's for headless=new with an explicit user-data-dir. The user's normal Chrome uses the "Google/Chrome" profile. pkill -f "Chrome-headless" is reasonably safe, but to be even safer, I'll only kill the PIDs from lsof :9222.)

One more thing: to be safe, add `--user-data-dir=/tmp/chrome-headless-prof` so it doesn't touch the real profile and to avoid the scoped_dir quarantine warnings. Also `--no-first-run --no-default-browser-check`.

